createApiClient() never uses the stored credentials URL (authConfig.url). After `os auth login --url ...`, running commands without --url will still default to OBJECTSTACK_URL or http://localhost:3000, which contradicts the documented priority order and breaks the “stored credentials” workflow. Consider loading authConfig when token is missing (or when url is missing) and using authConfig.url as the fallback before defaulting to localhost.
```suggestion
  // Resolve server URL (without applying defaults yet)
  let baseUrl = options.url || process.env.OBJECTSTACK_URL;

  // Resolve authentication token
  let token = options.token || process.env.OBJECTSTACK_TOKEN;

  // If URL or token is missing, try to load from stored credentials
  if (!baseUrl || !token) {
    try {
      const authConfig = await readAuthConfig();

      if (!token && authConfig.token) {
        token = authConfig.token;
      }

      if (!baseUrl && (authConfig as any).url) {
        baseUrl = (authConfig as any).url;
      }
    } catch {
      // No stored credentials - commands will fail if auth is required
    }
  }

  // Apply final default for baseUrl if still not resolved
  if (!baseUrl) {
    baseUrl = 'http://localhost:3000';
  }
```

This command bypasses `@objectstack/client` and hard-codes a DELETE request using `fetch`, while also reaching into private client fields (`(client as any).baseUrl/token`). That loses the client’s standardized error handling (and discovered route support) and increases maintenance cost. Prefer adding a `client.meta.deleteItem(type, name)` API to `@objectstack/client` (or exposing a safe request helper) and using that here.
```suggestion
      // Delegate to the client metadata API so we use standardized routing and error handling
      const metaClient = (client as any).meta;
      if (!metaClient || typeof metaClient.deleteItem !== 'function') {
        throw new Error('Metadata delete is not supported by the current client version.');
      }

      await metaClient.deleteItem(args.type, args.name);
```

The deleteAuthConfig tests use `vi.doMock('node:fs/promises', ...)` after `node:fs/promises` has already been imported/mocked at the top of the file. This doesn’t reliably affect the module instance used by deleteAuthConfig()’s dynamic import, and the tests currently don’t assert that `unlink` was called or that ENOENT is handled. Prefer configuring `vi.mocked(fs.unlink)` directly (and asserting calls) or using `vi.resetModules()` + re-import to ensure the mock applies.
```suggestion
      const mockUnlink = vi.mocked(fs.unlink);
      mockUnlink.mockResolvedValue(undefined as any);

      await deleteAuthConfig();

      expect(mockUnlink).toHaveBeenCalled();
      expect(mockUnlink).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
        expect.stringContaining('credentials.json')
      );
    });

    it('should not throw if file does not exist', async () => {
      const mockUnlink = vi.mocked(fs.unlink);
      mockUnlink.mockRejectedValue({ code: 'ENOENT' } as any);

      await expect(deleteAuthConfig()).resolves.not.toThrow();
      expect(mockUnlink).toHaveBeenCalled();
```
